  {
   "id": "google",
   "paragraph": "When an AI model answers a question about a long document, the computer needs to keep a lot of material close at hand. Think of working at a bigger desk so more pages stay within reach. Google announced two specialised versions of its TPU 8 hardware on November 9. The 8i Max gives each rack of computers more memory for jobs that use long documents or other large inputs. The 8tx uses encryption, which scrambles data to protect it, with features aimed at keeping sensitive information protected during processing. How either system performs on a team’s own tasks still needs to be measured.",
   "headline": "Google splits its AI chips by workload",
   "facts": [
    "Announced 9 Nov",
    "TPU 8i Max and TPU 8tx specialise the TPU 8 generation",
    "8i Max increases memory per rack for long-context inference",
    "8tx uses encryption features for confidential inference",
    "Workload-specific performance is an open question"
   ],
