   "mechanics": "Inflation rises when spending financed by income and credit outruns the quantity sold. If bond returns fail to keep up with prices, gold gives up less buying power relative to holding bonds.",
   "data": "Three forces matter. Payrolls rose by 165,000, supporting income and spending. The 10-year real yield rose to <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">2.66% on Monday</a>, increasing the cost of forgoing bond income. Monday’s breakeven eased to 2.22%, suggesting less inflation compensation. Unlike a debt-monetisation episode with falling real returns, the latest available real yield rose. The dollar and oil moves help explain why gold could rise anyway.",
