   "cycle": "The checklist still suggests a highly indebted phase: debt grew relative to output and annual revenue, while foreign Treasury holdings grew more slowly than a year earlier. A household spending more income on interest has less room for other bills. The comparison has limits: the government issues its own currency, and these interest and revenue observations are from different quarters. Negative yield-growth gaps using one annualised quarter do not establish sustainable financing or predict a crisis date.",
   "asset_classes": "Positive after-inflation Treasury yields compete with gold. If debt pressures eventually induce monetary easing and reduce those returns, gold can gain through that channel. Bonds also depend on inflation and repayment terms; nominal coupons do not guarantee buying power. Stocks depend on productivity and profits outrunning financing costs, which the current labour and output data alone cannot settle."
